[# Section 6 — the CX code language.
   Authored / expanded 2026-05-22 against the v0.8.0-dev branch.
   Anchored in spec/code.md (post-rename from programs.md), spec/cxpath_alignment.md,
   spec/cxdm.md §4.6 (EBV).
   Largest section in the guide. Cross-links use [[anchor]] form.
   File extension .cxd — data-only source. #]

[section id=code n=6 title='The code language'

  [child id=overview n=6.1 title='The CX code language'
    [intro
      """CX code is CX data with a leading `?` on the element name. A
         directive like `[?for]` is a normal element to the parser; the
         evaluator recognizes the `?` prefix and dispatches on the
         directive name. Everything else — patterns, paths, function
         calls, builtins, errors — is built from ordinary CX shapes."""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[# Data is data:                       #]
         [user id=1 email='ada@example.com']

         [# Code is data, plus a `?`:           #]
         [?for [in $u //user] [yield $u@email]]"""]

    [body
      """The evaluator is `cx_code_eval(doc, code, target_format)` at the
         C ABI level; every binding's Layer-1 `Doc.eval(code)` wraps it
         (see [[bindings]]). The full normative spec is
         [`spec/code.md`](../../spec/code.md). This section is the
         working developer guide."""]

    [body
      """Code can do five things: **select** (CXPath, see [[cxpath]]),
         **iterate** (`[?for]`, see [[for]]), **dispatch**
         (`[?match]`, see [[match]]), **bind** (`[?let]` / `[?def]` /
         `[?fn]`), and **update** (`[?modify]`, see [[modify]]). Every
         other directive is one of these five lifted into a specific
         domain — concurrency, async, resilience, services."""]

    [body
      """**Code is data.** A directive is an element whose name happens
         to start with `?`. You can pattern-match on `[?for ...]`
         exactly the way you pattern-match on `[user ...]`. You can
         transform code with `[?modify]`. You can hash it, project it
         to JSON, ship it across a wire, and evaluate it on the other
         end. The render rules in [`spec/code.md`](../../spec/code.md)
         §10.1 turn any well-formed program into a sequence diagram
         deterministically — because the program is just a tree."""]

    [body
      """**Pure-functional core.** Every directive returns a value;
         there is no assignment, no mutation, no rebinding. The
         concurrency primitives (channels, workers, async) exchange
         only immutable values, so there is no shared-mutable-state
         hazard by construction. See [[pure-updates]] for the
         lens/zipper foundation that `[?modify]` builds on."""]]

  [child id=directives n=6.2 title='Directive registry'
    [intro
      """v0.8.0 has **forty normative directives** — including
         `[?modify]`.
         Adding or removing any directive requires a governance change per
         [`spec/governance.md`](../../spec/governance.md) §10. The
         authoritative registry lives in
         [`spec/code.md`](../../spec/code.md) §4.1; this subsection is
         the at-a-glance grouping."""]

    [body
      """**Core constructs (11).** Built into the evaluator;
         everything else composes from these:"""]

    [table columns=(directive, purpose)
      [row directive='[?match]' purpose='Pattern dispatch — single-arm assertion or multi-arm first-match (see [[match]])']
      [row directive='[?modify]' purpose='Pure-functional update — apply an action at every CXPath focus (see [[modify]])']
      [row directive='[?for]' purpose='Iteration / comprehension over a CXPath, pattern, or sequence (see [[for]])']
      [row directive='[?let]' purpose='Local binding scoped to a body (see [[let]])']
      [row directive='[?fn]' purpose='Anonymous function literal — body of $bindings → expression']
      [row directive='[?def]' purpose='Named top-level definition (function or value)']
      [row directive='[?if]' purpose='Boolean branch with EBV condition (see [[if]] + [[ebv]])']
      [row directive='[?else]' purpose='Value-or-default coalesce — recover on [err] or absence (getOrElse; see [[errors]])']
      [row directive='[?pipe]' purpose='Pipeline — canonical form of the `|` operator (see [[pipe]])']
      [row directive='[?map]' purpose='Map a closure over a sequence (sequential by default; par=true enables parallel evaluation, ordered=true preserves source order)']
      [row directive='[?reduce]' purpose='Fold a sequence to a single value via a binary closure starting from [init …] (sequential left-fold by default; par=true requires associativity)']]

    [body
      """**Resilience (6).** Wrap an inner expression with a
         failure-handling policy. See [[resilience]] for the parameter
         tables:"""]

    [table columns=(directive, purpose)
      [row directive='[?retry]' purpose='Re-run on failure with backoff + jitter; bounded by max=']
      [row directive='[?timeout]' purpose='Bound execution time; cancel + return [on-timeout …] on expiry']
      [row directive='[?circuit-breaker]' purpose='Open after threshold-exceeded failures in a rolling window']
      [row directive='[?fallback]' purpose='Run [recover-with …] secondary if primary returns [err …] ($err bound in the recovery)']
      [row directive='[?rate-limit]' purpose='Token-bucket cap — max= calls per per= duration']
      [row directive='[?bulkhead]' purpose='Bound max-concurrent= in-flight; optional [queue …]']]

    [body
      """**Services + clients (3).** HTTP service definition + client
         construction. See [[services]]:"""]

    [table columns=(directive, purpose)
      [row directive='[?service]' purpose='Declare an HTTP service with resource handlers; returns a handle']
      [row directive='[?service-handle]' purpose='Look up an existing service by :name']
      [row directive='[?http-client]' purpose='Construct an HTTP client (optionally wrapped in resilience)']]

    [body
      """**Concurrency (10).** Workers + channels + selection. See
         [[workers]]:"""]

    [table columns=(directive, purpose)
      [row directive='[?worker]' purpose='Spawn a worker; concurrent with siblings in the enclosing scope']
      [row directive='[?worker-handle]' purpose='Look up an existing worker by :name']
      [row directive='[?channel]' purpose='Create a FIFO channel with :buffer of given size']
      [row directive='[?send]' purpose='Blocking send to a channel; returns [ok] or CHANNEL_CLOSED']
      [row directive='[?try-send]' purpose='Send with :timeout — SEND_TIMEOUT if buffer stays full']
      [row directive='[?receive]' purpose='Blocking receive — returns next value or CHANNEL_CLOSED']
      [row directive='[?try-receive]' purpose='Receive with :timeout — RECV_TIMEOUT if empty']
      [row directive='[?close]' purpose='Close a channel; second close raises CHANNEL_ALREADY_CLOSED']
      [row directive='[?select]' purpose='Multi-way receive — first ready case wins (uniform-random on ties)']
      [row directive='[?stop]' purpose='Initiate graceful shutdown on a service or worker handle']]

    [body
      """**Async (8).** Futures + barriers + cancellation. See
         [[async]]:"""]

    [table columns=(directive, purpose)
      [row directive='[?async]' purpose='Evaluate EXPR asynchronously; return a future immediately']
      [row directive='[?await]' purpose='Block until a future is terminal; return value or [err]']
      [row directive='[?await-all]' purpose='Join N futures — all-or-error barrier']
      [row directive='[?await-any]' purpose='Yield first to terminate; others continue running']
      [row directive='[?await-race]' purpose='Yield first to terminate; cancel the rest']
      [row directive='[?cancel]' purpose='Request cooperative cancellation on a future/worker handle']
      [row directive='[?check-cancel]' purpose='Polling cancellation point inside a CPU-bound loop']
      [row directive='[?sleep]' purpose='Cancellable sleep for DURATION']]

    [body
      """**Lifecycle (2 shared).** `[?stop]` (above) and
         `[?wait-for]` — block until a service or worker terminates,
         returning its terminal value or `[err]`."""]

    [note
      """**Retired in v0.8.0.** `[?find]` is no longer a directive. Use
         `[?for]` with a pattern generator, or CXPath `//pattern`. See
         [[migration]] for mechanical migration rules. The rationale:
         CXPath is the universal selector, so the
         `[?find]` shape was redundant with `[?for]`+CXPath."""]

    [note
      """**Implementation status.** Multi-arm `[?match]`,
         `[?modify]`, and the CXPath value kind in
         constructs other than `[?for]` are **fully
         specified** but not all are wired through every binding
         yet. V (the native reference) carries the spec-conformant
         implementation; Tier-2 bindings (Python, Go, Rust)
         follow per the parity matrix in
         [`spec/v0_8_0_status.md`](../../spec/v0_8_0_status.md). Where
         a feature is spec'd-but-not-shipped this guide says so
         explicitly."""]]

  [child id=cxpath n=6.3 title='CXPath — the universal selector'
    [intro
      """CXPath is the single selector vocabulary across reading,
         iteration, dispatch, and update. It aligns with XPath 3.1
         (see [`spec/cxpath_alignment.md`](../../spec/cxpath_alignment.md))
         with deliberate divergences kept minimal. **Path is a value
         kind**, not parser sugar — paths compile, cache, round-trip
         through every projection, and visualize as themselves."""]

    [body
      """The simplest CXPath is the descendant search:"""]

    [example lang=cx
      """//user                       [# every user, anywhere in doc      #]
         //user[@active=true]         [# every active user                #]
         //user/@email                [# scalar attribute values          #]
         //section[1]                 [# first section (1-indexed)        #]
         //section[h2]                [# section that has an h2 child     #]"""]

    [body
      """The grammar is the XPath 3.1 **selection core**, lifted into
         CX with one comparison operator family:"""]

    [example lang=cx
      """PathExpr   ::= '//' StepList | '/' StepList | StepList
         Step       ::= ( Axis '::' )? NodeTest Predicate*
         Predicate  ::= '[' PredExpr ']'
         PredExpr   ::= AttrTest | INT | FunctionCall | BooleanExpr"""]

    [child id=cxpath-axes n=6.3.1 title='The twelve axes'
      [body
        """CXPath supports twelve axes. The default axis is `child`,
           so `name` ≡ `child::name`. The full set:"""]
      [table columns=(axis, direction, what-it-selects)
        [row axis='child' direction='down' what-it-selects='Direct children of context node (default axis)']
        [row axis='descendant' direction='down' what-it-selects='Children, grandchildren, deeper — excludes self']
        [row axis='descendant-or-self' direction='down' what-it-selects='`descendant` plus the context node itself']
        [row axis='parent' direction='up' what-it-selects='The unique parent of context (or empty at root)']
        [row axis='ancestor' direction='up' what-it-selects='Parent, grandparent, up to root — excludes self']
        [row axis='ancestor-or-self' direction='up' what-it-selects='`ancestor` plus the context node itself']
        [row axis='following-sibling' direction='lateral' what-it-selects='Later siblings with same parent']
        [row axis='preceding-sibling' direction='lateral' what-it-selects='Earlier siblings with same parent']
        [row axis='following' direction='document' what-it-selects='Every node after context in document order (excluding descendants)']
        [row axis='preceding' direction='document' what-it-selects='Every node before context in document order (excluding ancestors)']
        [row axis='self' direction='self' what-it-selects='Context node itself — useful for predicate filters']
        [row axis='attribute' direction='attr' what-it-selects='Attributes of context — shorthand is `@name`']]
      [example lang=cx
        """[# Common axis idioms                                              #]
           //section//*                              [# all descendants       #]
           //code/ancestor::section                   [# enclosing sections    #]
           //user/following-sibling::user             [# later peer users      #]
           //tr/parent::table                         [# tables containing tr  #]
           //h2/preceding-sibling::*[1]               [# immediate prior sib   #]
           //user[self::admin]                        [# only-if-admin filter  #]
           //user/attribute::email                    [# == //user/@email      #]"""]
      [body
        """All twelve axes are reachable from any binding too — `$u`
           plus an axis-qualified step navigates from that value:
           `$u/ancestor::section` walks upward from `$u`. See
           [[bindings]]."""]]

    [child id=cxpath-predicates n=6.3.2 title='Predicates — positional, attribute, function, composite'
      [body
        """A predicate is `[...]` after a step. CXPath recognises four
           predicate shapes; mixed forms compose:"""]
      [list
        [item """**Positional** — `INT` selects the Nth match
                (1-indexed). `//section[1]` is the first section.
                Negative positions are NOT supported; use
                `[last()]` for the last."""]
        [item """**Attribute** — `@name`, `@name=value`,
                `@name!=value`, `@name<value` etc. Tests an
                attribute's value. Sigil shortcut: `[+active]`
                ≡ `[@active=true]`, `[-banned]` ≡ `[@banned=false]`."""]
        [item """**Function** — any boolean-returning builtin or
                user-defined function: `[contains(@name, 'Ada')]`,
                `[starts-with(@email, 'a')]`, `[count(*) > 0]`."""]
        [item """**Composite** — boolean combinations with `and` /
                `or` / `not`: `[@active=true and @region='EU']`,
                `[@status='ok' or @status='warn']`."""]]
      [example lang=cx
        """//user[1]                              [# first user                #]
           //user[@id=42]                         [# attr equality             #]
           //user[+active]                        [# sigil shorthand           #]
           //user[contains(@email, '@example.com')]
           //user[@age >= 18 and @country='US']
           //section[h2 and p]                    [# has both h2 and p children#]"""]
      [body
        """Predicates chain: `//user[@active=true][1]` is the first
           active user — the second predicate filters the result of
           the first. The order matters."""]]

    [child id=cxpath-comparison n=6.3.3 title='Comparison — one operator family'
      [body
        """**One operator syntax: `= != < <= > >=`.** No keyword
           synonyms (`eq` / `ne` / `lt` / ...) — they are not in the
           grammar. Semantics are **value-comparison**: both sides
           must be single values; a multi-valued operand raises
           `CXER0103`; no type coercion."""]
      [body
        """This is the deliberate divergence from XPath 3.1's
           general-comparison default. In XPath, `//x = 1` is true if
           *any* `x` equals 1 — a common gotcha when the path
           silently returns multiple nodes. In CX, the path must
           return a single value, or the comparison raises. To
           express "any equals", use the explicit builtin:
           `some(//x, fn($v){ $v = 1 })`."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """//user[@age = 42]              [# single attr → ok               #]
           //section[count(p) > 3]        [# both sides single → ok         #]
           //user[@email = 'ada@…']       [# single string equality         #]
           [# //user[@tags = 'admin']     # raises if @tags is multi-valued #]
           //user[some(@tags, fn($t){ $t = 'admin' })]"""]]

    [child id=cxpath-bindings n=6.3.4 title='Paths on bindings'
      [body
        """A CXPath rooted at a binding navigates from that value
           instead of from the document root:"""]
      [example lang=cx
        """$u/name                  [# direct child element               #]
           $u/*                     [# all direct children                #]
           $u//item                 [# descendant                         #]
           $u/ancestor::section     [# explicit axis                      #]
           $u@active                [# attribute access (shorthand)       #]
           $u/@active               [# attribute access (full)            #]
           $u.region                [# map-key access (when $u is a map)  #]"""]
      [body
        """The path operators that work on bindings are the same
           operators that work on the implicit document — one
           vocabulary. The leading `//` form is anchored at the
           **document root**, regardless of context; `$u//x` is
           anchored at `$u`. Use `//x` to escape upward when you
           already have a binding."""]]

    [child id=cxpath-context n=6.3.5 title='Where CXPath values are accepted'
      [body
        """CXPath is a value kind. Every construct that takes "a
           selector" accepts a CXPath value:"""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?for [in $u //user[@active=true]] [yield [$render $u]]]
           [?if //admin [then [admin-panel]] [else ()]]
           [?match $n [case //prose [yield [p $n]]]]
           [?let [= $cfg //meta/@config] [$render $cfg]]
           [?modify $doc //user/@status [set 'verified']]
           [$count //user[@active=true]]"""]
      [body
        """The same path expression — `//user[@active=true]` —
           appears as a `[?for]` generator, as an `[?if]` condition,
           as a `[?match]` arm, as a `[?let]` initializer, as a
           `[?modify]` focus, and as a builtin argument. This is the
           unification CX ratifies: no separate "path syntax"
           per construct."""]
      [body
        """**Status note.** CXPath value-kind at non-`[?for]`
           positions is production-ready in V; full Tier-2 binding parity is
           tracked in
           [`spec/v0_8_0_status.md`](../../spec/v0_8_0_status.md)
           gate 26.4."""]]

    [child id=cxpath-divergences n=6.3.6 title='Deliberate divergences from XPath 3.1'
      [body
        """CXPath is a working subset of XPath 3.1 + deliberate
           changes. The full alignment table is in
           [`spec/cxpath_alignment.md`](../../spec/cxpath_alignment.md);
           the headline differences:"""]
      [list
        [item """**One comparison family** (`= != < <= > >=`) —
                XPath's split into general (`=`) and value (`eq`) is
                gone; CX uses value-comparison semantics
                exclusively. Rationale: the general-comparison
                gotcha is XPath's most-reported source of subtle
                bugs."""]
        [item """**Path is a value kind** — XPath paths are syntactic
                fragments inside XPath expressions only. In CX they
                are first-class values."""]
        [item """**1-indexed positions** — adopted from XPath
                verbatim (`//x[1]` is the first), but CX explicitly
                rejects `[0]` (raises `CXER0100`); XPath silently
                yields the empty sequence."""]
        [item """**No general-purpose `for` / `let` inside paths** —
                CX moves binding into the `[?for]` / `[?let]`
                directive surface; predicates take expressions but
                not binding clauses. Use the surrounding directive."""]
        [item """**No XPath functions in head position** — bare
                `count(//user)` parses at expression top-level (a
                builtin call) but not inside a path step;
                `[count(*) > 0]` works because it is a predicate
                expression."""]]
      [note
        """The full XPath 3.1 alignment table — what is adopted, what
           is deliberately not, what is on the XPath 4.0 watch list —
           is in
           [`spec/cxpath_alignment.md`](../../spec/cxpath_alignment.md)."""]]]

  [child id=patterns n=6.4 title='Patterns and matching'
    [intro
      """A **pattern** is a literal CX subtree extended with binding
         holes (`$name`), wildcards (`*`, `**`, `_`), type guards
         (`:TypeName`), scalar literals, and CXPath values. Patterns
         appear in `[?for]`'s first generator, in `[?match]` arms,
         and in destructuring `[?let]`. The full pattern grammar is
         in [`spec/code.md`](../../spec/code.md) §5."""]

    [child id=pattern-element-shape n=6.4.1 title='Element-shape patterns'
      [body
        """An element-shape pattern matches an element by name,
           attributes, and (recursively) body content. The pattern
           uses ordinary CX syntax with bind-holes where you want
           to capture values:"""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[user email=$e]               [# bind email attribute             #]
           [user @active=true $u]        [# attr filter; bind whole user     #]
           [user @id=42 [name $n]]       [# bind name's body                 #]
           [post author=$a body=$b]      [# bind two attrs                   #]"""]
      [body
        """**Binding semantics depend on context.** A pattern
           `[NAME $x]` **without** attribute predicates auto-unwraps
           the body — text body binds the string, scalar body binds
           the scalar, empty body binds the element itself,
           structured body binds the element. A pattern
           `[NAME @attr… $x]` **with** attribute predicates always
           binds `$x` to the matched element. The rationale is in
           [`spec/code.md`](../../spec/code.md) §5.2 rule 5: when
           you name attributes you are filtering "what kind of
           element", so the bind value is the element; when you
           don't, the bind value is the inner content."""]]

    [child id=pattern-scalar n=6.4.2 title='Scalar literal patterns'
      [body
        """Scalar literals are valid patterns. They match by
           value-equality:"""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?match $status
           [case 200    :ok]
           [case 404    :not-found]
           [case 500    :server-error]
           [case 'admin' :elevated]
           [case true   :enabled]
           [case null   :missing]
           [else        :unknown]]"""]
      [body
        """Integers, floats, strings, bools, dates, datetimes, and
           `null` are all matchable as scalar literals. Float
           equality follows the EBV rules — `nan` is **never** equal
           to `nan` (per IEEE-754). Use `is-nan($x)` to test for NaN
           explicitly."""]]

    [child id=pattern-cxpath n=6.4.3 title='CXPath patterns'
      [body
        """A CXPath value used as a pattern matches if the
           candidate value is in the path's result sequence:"""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?match $node
           [case //heading [h2 $node]]
           [case //prose   [p $node]]
           [else           $node]]"""]
      [body
        """In a `[?for]` body, the same CXPath as the generator
           selects directly:"""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?for [in $u //user[@active=true]] [yield $u@email]]"""]
      [body
        """CXPath patterns and element-shape patterns compose in
           the same arm list — `[?match]` can dispatch on a mix of
           both, with first-match-wins ordering (see [[match]])."""]]

    [child id=pattern-wildcards n=6.4.4 title='Wildcards — *, **, _'
      [body
        """Three wildcard forms:"""]
      [list
        [item """**`*` — any single element.** `[* $x]` matches any
                element and binds it. `[section * $body]` matches
                any section and binds the body wildcard slot."""]
        [item """**`**` — recursive descent.** `[**]` matches any
                element at this position or deeper. Combined with
                a name: `[**user]` matches any `user` anywhere in
                the body."""]
        [item """**`_` — anything, no bind.** `_` matches any value
                (including non-elements) without binding it. The
                idiomatic catch-all in `[?match]`."""]]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?match $node
           [case [section * $body] [$process-section $body]]
           [case [**code $c]       [$highlight $c]]
           [case _                 $node]]"""]]

    [child id=pattern-binding n=6.4.5 title='Named bindings — $name'
      [body
        """`$name` (lowercase, leading `$`) is a binding hole. It
           appears in attribute-value position
           (`[user email=$e]`), in body position (`[user $u]`),
           and in destructuring-let position
           (`[?let [user @id=1 $admin] = …]`)."""]
      [body
        """A bind hole always succeeds at its position; the
           bind value depends on what the surrounding pattern
           dictates (see [[pattern-element-shape]]). Naming
           collisions inside one arm raise `CXER0100`
           (PARSE_ERROR) — `[user @id=$x @name=$x]` would require
           the values be equal, which the v0.8.0 spec does not
           support; use a single bind and check with a `[when …]` clause."""]]

    [child id=pattern-type-guards n=6.4.6 title='Type-guard heads'
      [body
        """A type guard `:TypeName` at element-head position
           narrows by schema-typed shape:"""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[# Match only elements with declared schema type ServerConfig    #]
           [?match $cfg
             [case [* :ServerConfig $c] [$validate-server $c]]
             [case [* :ClientConfig $c] [$validate-client $c]]
             [else [result status=err code='CXER0103' reason='unknown config']]]"""]
      [body
        """Type-guard resolution is by the document's schema
           binding (see [[schema]] in the data section). When no
           schema is in scope the guard fails by default; pair
           with schema-on-validate (`[?cx schema='…']`) for
           type-driven dispatch."""]]]

  [child id=bindings n=6.5 title='Bindings and paths'
    [intro
      """A binding is a name introduced by `[?let]`, `[?for]`,
         `[?fn]`, or `[?match]` pattern. Bindings are referenced via
         `$name`. They are immutable — there is no rebinding (per
         CX's pure-functional discipline)."""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[?let [= $admins //user[@role='admin']]
            [?for [in $a $admins] [yield $a@email]]]"""]

    [body
      """A binding's value is whatever expression produced it:
         a sequence, a scalar, an element, an array, a map, an
         error, a future, a CXPath, a function literal. Every value
         is a sequence (per the sequence-flat principle); single
         values are sequences of one."""]

    [body
      """**Navigation from a binding** uses CXPath syntax (see
         [[cxpath-bindings]]). The path operators that work on
         bindings are the same operators that work on the implicit
         document — one vocabulary. `$u/email` is a child step;
         `$u@email` is the attribute shorthand; `$u/@email` is the
         full attribute axis form."""]

    [body
      """**Scope.** A binding is in scope inside the directive
         body that introduced it. `[?let [= $x E] BODY]` puts
         `$x` in scope for `BODY` only; outside the let it does
         not exist. `[?for [in $u SEQ] [yield E]]` puts `$u` in
         scope for `E` (one iteration at a time, per item)."""]

    [body
      """**Shadowing.** A nested binding with the same name shadows
         the outer for the inner scope; this is the standard lexical
         rule and matches every host language. CX does not warn on
         shadowing — readability is the author's responsibility."""]]

  [child id=constructs n=6.6 title='Core constructs'
    [intro
      """The six core constructs are how every CX program is built.
         Each is documented in the following sub-sections."""]

    [child id=for n=6.6.1 title='[?for] — iteration'
      [body
        """`[?for]` is the iteration / comprehension primitive. The
           expression in the `[in …]` clause is either a CXPath
           (yields a sequence to iterate), a pattern (matches the
           generator — the `[?find]` replacement), or any expression
           that evaluates to a sequence."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[# CXPath generator                                           #]
           [?for [in $u //user] [yield $u@email]]

           [# Pattern generator (the `[?find]` replacement)              #]
           [?for [user @active=true $u] [yield $u@email]]

           [# Filter with [where …]                                      #]
           [?for [in $u //user] [where [>= $u@age 18]] [yield $u]]

           [# Multiple generators (Cartesian, in declared order)         #]
           [?for [in $u //user]
                 [in $p //post[@author=$u@id]]
                 [yield [tuple $u $p]]]

           [# Parallel evaluation                                        #]
           [?for [in $r //request] par=true [yield [$process $r]]]

           [# Streaming over large input                                 #]
           [?for [in $row //logs/row] stream=true [yield [$extract $row]]]"""]
      [body
        """`par=true` and `stream=true` are evaluation modifiers — same
           surface, different scheduler. `par=true` distributes
           iterations across worker threads (preserving output
           order). `stream=true` couples the generator to a streaming
           input source so that the iteration runs in bounded
           memory regardless of input size. Full semantics in
           [`spec/code.md`](../../spec/code.md) §7."""]
      [body
        """**Multiple `[where …]` clauses** AND together; **multiple
           `[yield …]` clauses** concatenate (per the sequence-flat
           principle — yields never produce a list-of-lists)."""]]

    [child id=match n=6.6.2 title='[?match] — dispatch (single + multi-arm)'
      [body
        """`[?match]` is pattern dispatch. v0.8.0 supports two
           forms: **2-arg destructure** (single arm, asserts the
           value matches or raises) and **multi-arm** (first-match-
           wins across N arms). The multi-arm form is the major
           addition for v0.8.0."""]

      [body
        """**Single-arm form** — pattern-bind a value and assert
           the shape:"""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[# Destructures $result as [ok V] or raises CXER0100 #]
           [?match $result [ok $v] [yield [$render $v]]]"""]

      [body
        """**Multi-arm form** — first-match-wins across `[case …]` /
           `[when …]` / `[else …]` clauses. Arms may mix element-shape
           patterns, scalar literals, CXPath paths, wildcards,
           and predicate-only `[when …]` clauses:"""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?match $node
           [case [prose $p]   [p $p]]
           [case [code $c]    [pre $c]]
           [case //heading    [h2 $node]]
           [case 'separator'  [hr]]
           [case _            ()]]

           [# SQL Searched-CASE — predicate-only mode (no scrutinee)     #]
           [?match
             [when [> $x 100]   :big]
             [when [> $y 50]    :medium]
             [else              :small]]

           [# Guards on case arms — [where …] filters even on a match    #]
           [?match $req
             [case [http-get $u] [where [$allowed $u]] [route $u]]
             [case [http-get $u]                       [403 forbidden]]
             [else                                     [400 bad-request]]]"""]

      [body
        """**Arm semantics:**"""]
      [list
        [item """**`[case PAT R]` — match against pattern `PAT`.**
                Arms evaluate top-to-bottom; the first one whose
                pattern matches "wins". Later arms are not tried,
                even if they would also have matched."""]
        [item """**`[when PRED R]` — match iff predicate truthy.**
                No pattern; the predicate is evaluated with the
                scrutinee in scope as `$_` (and any earlier `[case
                …]` bindings still in scope only if the same arm)."""]
        [item """**`[else R]` — catch-all, always last.** Equivalent
                to `[case _ R]`. Multiple `[else …]` clauses raise
                `CXER0100`."""]
        [item """**`[case PAT [where PRED] R]` — guard on a case
                arm.** The pattern must match AND the predicate
                must be truthy."""]
        [item """**No implicit EBV in `[case …]`.** `[case …]` tests
                pattern equality / structural match; it does not
                apply EBV. `[when …]` uses EBV."""]]

      [body
        """**No-match behavior differs by form.** The 2-arg form
           raises `CXER0100`; the multi-arm form yields `()` (the
           empty sequence) if no arm matches and no `[else …]` is
           present. An unreachable arm (covered by an earlier arm
           with no guard) raises a warning at parse time but does
           not fail compilation."""]

      [note
        """**Status.** Multi-arm `[?match]` is fully spec'd.
           V's evaluator carries the production-ready
           implementation; Tier-2 binding parity is per
           [`spec/v0_8_0_status.md`](../../spec/v0_8_0_status.md)."""]]

    [child id=if n=6.6.3 title='[?if] — boolean branch (with EBV)'
      [body
        """`[?if cond [then E1] [else E2]]` is the explicit boolean
           branch. The condition is evaluated with the EBV
           (Effective Boolean Value) rule — see [[ebv]] below."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?if //admin [then [admin-panel]] [else [user-panel]]]
           [?if [>= $age 18] [then :adult] [else :minor]]
           [?if @debug [then [$debug-trace]] [else ()]]"""]
      [body
        """For multi-way conditionals prefer `[?match]` multi-arm
           (see [[match]]). `[?if]` stays separate from `[?match]`
           because they have different contracts — `[?if]` uses
           EBV (truthy/falsy with container rules), `[?match]`
           `[case …]` does **not** (it tests strictly by structural
           equality / pattern match)."""]
      [body
        """**The `[else …]` clause is optional.** When omitted, an
           untrue condition yields `()` (the empty sequence). Many
           CX idioms exploit this — `[?if @hidden [then ()] [else
           [$render ...]]]` reads as "render unless hidden"."""]

      [child id=ebv n=6.6.3.1 title='EBV — the Effective Boolean Value rule'
        [body
          """The EBV rule, applied in order (per
             [`spec/cxdm.md`](../../spec/cxdm.md) §4.6):"""]
        [list
          [item """**Empty sequence `()`** → false."""]
          [item """**Single Scalar** of kind `bool` → that bool's
                  value."""]
          [item """**Single Scalar** of kind `string` →
                  length > 0."""]
          [item """**Single Scalar** of kind `int` / `float` →
                  value `!= 0` (and not `nan` for float)."""]
          [item """**Single Scalar** of kind `null` → false."""]
          [item """**Single Scalar** of kind `date` / `datetime` /
                  `bytes` → true (existence is truthy)."""]
          [item """**Single Node (element)** → true."""]
          [item """**Single Array** (v1.1 / v0.8.0) → true iff
                  non-empty (length > 0)."""]
          [item """**Single Map** (v1.1) → true iff non-empty."""]
          [item """**Sequence of length > 1** → true."""]]
        [body
          """This is the rule that makes `[?if //service …]`
             mean "if any service exists", `[?if @debug …]` mean
             "if the debug attribute exists and is truthy", and
             `[?if @items …]` mean "if the items array is
             non-empty" when `@items` is an Array-typed
             attribute."""]
        [body
          """The container rules (Array/Map empty-is-falsy)
             follow the convention shared by Python lists/dicts,
             JSON-template engines, and YAML/TOML processors.
             CX deliberately diverges from XPath 3.1 here, which
             raises a type error on EBV(array); CX's pragmatic
             rule reads more naturally in template position."""]]]

    [child id=let n=6.6.4 title='[?let] — binding'
      [body
        """`[?let]` introduces a local binding. The bound name is
           in scope inside the body that follows."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?let [= $config //meta/@config]
             [$render $config]]

           [# Multiple bindings — sequential, each sees the prior  #]
           [?let [= $base //meta/@host]
                 [= $port //meta/@port]
                 [= $url  [$concat 'https://' $base ':' $port]]
                 [http-client target=$url]]

           [# Destructuring let — pattern in binding position      #]
           [?let [= [user @id=1 $admin] //user[@id=1]]
             $admin@email]"""]
      [body
        """`[?let]` is the only way to give an expression a name
           in v0.8.0 — there is no `def` inside a function body,
           no inline `where` (that is a `[?for]` filter). To name
           a value that is used multiple times, `[?let]` is
           idiomatic."""]]

    [child id=modify n=6.6.5 title='[?modify] — pure-functional updates'
      [body
        """`[?modify]` is the v0.8.0 update directive. It takes a
           document (or sub-tree), a CXPath focus, and one or
           more action slots; it returns a **new** document with
           the action applied at every focus match. The original
           is unchanged (pure-functional)."""]

      [body
        """The **eleven action slots** are:"""]

      [table columns=(action, description)
        [row action='[set V]' description='Replace matched value, element body, or attribute value with V']
        [row action='[delete]' description='Remove matched node from its parent body (or attribute from element)']
        [row action='[using F]' description='Apply function F to each match; F receives the match, returns the replacement']
        [row action='[rename N]' description='Rename matched element (preserves attrs + body)']
        [row action='[set-attr N V]' description='Add new attribute N=V, or overwrite if N already present']
        [row action='[delete-attr N]' description='Remove attribute N from matched element']
        [row action='[append V]' description='Add child V at end of matched body']
        [row action='[prepend V]' description='Add child V at start of matched body']
        [row action='[insert-before V]' description='Insert V as a sibling immediately before each match']
        [row action='[insert-after V]' description='Insert V as a sibling immediately after each match']
        [row action='[replace V]' description='Replace entire matched node (including its surrounding placement) with V']]

      [example lang=cx
        """[# Scalar attribute update                                    #]
           [?modify $doc //user[@id=1]/@name [set 'Alice']]

           [# Delete matching nodes                                      #]
           [?modify $doc //user[@active=false] [delete]]

           [# Transform with [using …]                                   #]
           [?modify $doc //price [using [?fn ($p) [* $p 1.1]]]]

           [# Rename — keep attrs + body, change name                    #]
           [?modify $doc //para [rename p]]

           [# Set + delete attrs                                         #]
           [?modify $doc //user [set-attr verified true]]
           [?modify $doc //user [delete-attr legacy-id]]

           [# Append + prepend children                                  #]
           [?modify $doc //section[@id='intro'] [append [para 'Added.']]]
           [?modify $doc //list [prepend [item 'header item']]]

           [# Insert siblings                                            #]
           [?modify $doc //h2 [insert-before [hr]]]
           [?modify $doc //code [insert-after [aside 'note']]]

           [# Whole-node replace                                         #]
           [?modify $doc //deprecated [replace ()]]

           [# Multi-step pipeline composition (see [[pipe]])             #]
           [?pipe $doc
             [?modify //user/@status [set 'verified']]
             [?modify //user[@banned=true] [delete]]
             [?modify //user [set-attr last-audit '2026-05-22']]]"""]

      [body
        """**Pure-functional — structural sharing.** `[?modify]`
           does not copy the whole tree. Only the spine from root
           to each matched node is copied; unchanged branches are
           shared with the original (immutable persistent data
           structure). For a 10 MB document with one matched node,
           the new heap cost is < 1 KB per matched node — and the
           original `$doc` value is still valid for read or hash."""]

      [body
        """**Multi-match.** The focus path selects a sequence; the
           action applies to every node in that sequence. Zero
           matches returns the original unchanged (not an error —
           the no-match-no-op invariant lets pipelines compose
           safely). All-matches-at-once means there is no
           ordering hazard between matches."""]

      [body
        """**Identity invariant.** `[?modify $doc //x [set v]]`
           where `//x` matches no nodes returns `$doc` unchanged
           — same canonical bytes, same SHA-256. `[?modify $doc
           //x [delete]]` where every `//x` is removed returns a
           value with `count(//x) = 0`. Round-trip through
           ast-bin / data-bin / JSON preserves the post-modify
           shape exactly."""]

      [note
        """**Status.** `[?modify]` is fully spec'd.
           The V evaluator carries the production-ready
           implementation. Tier-2 binding parity is tracked in
           [`spec/v0_8_0_status.md`](../../spec/v0_8_0_status.md)
           gate 30."""]]

    [child id=pipe n=6.6.6 title='Pipelines and composition'
      [body
        """The `[?pipe]` directive threads the output of one
           expression into the input of the next. Pipelines
           compose `[?modify]`, `[?for]`, builtins, and
           user-defined functions into readable top-to-bottom
           data flow."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?pipe //user[@active=true]
           [$filter-by-region _ 'EU']
           [$sort-by _ @last-login]
           [$take _ 10]]"""]
      [body
        """**Stages are bare transforms.** `[?pipe SRC STAGE …]`
           evaluates `SRC`, then supplies the value to each `STAGE`
           left-to-right. A stage is a transform — a bare name, a
           `$ref`, or a `[$call …]` — never wrapped in a keyword
           (there is no `[through]` clause). `[?pipe 5 add1 add1]`
           → `7`."""]
      [body
        """**Stage invocation (§8.9.1).** How the value is supplied
           depends on the stage shape: a **hole-form** stage carries
           a single partial-application hole `_` and the value fills
           it (`[$get _ '/users/42']` → `get(client, '/users/42')`);
           a **no-hole** stage receives the value **appended as its
           final positional argument** (`count` ≡ `[$count _]`). A
           pipe threads exactly one value, so a stage may carry **at
           most one** `_` (two or more → `cx-err:CXER0100`). A
           non-callable stage (a literal, map, or data element) is
           `cx-err:CXER0100` — it never silently passes the value
           through."""]
      [body
        """**Fan-out behavior.** When `SRC` evaluates to a sequence,
           a stage sees the whole sequence as a single value (the
           sequence-flat principle — no implicit per-item dispatch).
           For a per-item transform, thread a `[?fn]` lambda that
           runs the comprehension:"""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?pipe //user
           [?fn ($us) [?for [in $u $us] [yield [$decorate $u]]]]
           [$sort-by _ @name]]"""]
      [body
        """**`[tap f]` — observe without diverting (§8.9.2).** A
           `[tap f]` stage applies `f` to the current value for its
           **side effect**; the value passes through unchanged and
           `f`'s return is discarded. Inside a stage, `_` names the
           current value, so `[tap [$log:info 'seen' fields={v: _}]]`
           logs and flows on. A tap propagates an `[err]` only when
           its code is `CXER0001` (panic) or `CXER0260`
           (cancellation); every other error is discarded for the
           data flow (the error-hook still observes it)."""]
      [body
        """**Railway short-circuit (§8.9.3).** The pipe is a
           single-channel failure railway: the first stage to yield
           `[err …]` skips every remaining stage — taps included —
           and that `[err]` is the result. An empty result is **not**
           a short-circuit; it flows to the next stage. There is no
           infix `|` and no `[through]` wrapper — both retired."""]]]

  [child id=builtins n=6.7 title='Built-in functions'
    [intro
      """Built-ins are the standard library at the expression
         layer. They are pure-functional, total over their
         declared input types, and stable across bindings.
         Full normative table in
         [`spec/code.md`](../../spec/code.md) §6.5; the
         subsections below organize the ~80 names by category."""]

    [body
      """**Invocation.** Every call uses the
         bracket head-dispatch form: `[$name args…]` for the
         $-bound surface, or `[prefix/name args…]` for
         module-qualified calls. The legacy paren form
         `name(args)` is being retired. The whitespace-separated
         element form `[name a b]` without a leading `$` is
         **always** element construction; write `[$count //user]`
         (call) versus `[count //user]` (data element)."""]

    [child id=sequence-builtins n=6.7.1 title='Sequence built-ins'
      [body
        """Operate on a Sequence. Argument may be a CX program
           sequence, the implicit document, or a single
           non-sequence value (treated as a 1-element
           sequence)."""]
      [table columns=(builtin, arity, semantics)
        [row builtin='count(seq)' arity='1' semantics='Integer count; scalar input yields 1; alias: length(seq)']
        [row builtin='length(seq)' arity='1' semantics='Synonym for count']
        [row builtin='empty(seq)' arity='1' semantics='Boolean — true iff count(seq) == 0']
        [row builtin='exists(seq)' arity='1' semantics='Boolean — true iff count(seq) > 0; opposite of empty']
        [row builtin='first(seq)' arity='1' semantics='First item; scalar passes through; XQuery fn:head parity']
        [row builtin='last(seq)' arity='1' semantics='Last item; scalar passes through']
        [row builtin='head(seq)' arity='1' semantics='Synonym for first']
        [row builtin='tail(seq)' arity='1' semantics='All items except first; scalar yields ()']
        [row builtin='reverse(seq)' arity='1' semantics='Items in reverse order; scalar passes through']
        [row builtin='distinct(seq)' arity='1' semantics='Items with structural duplicates removed; first-occurrence order']
        [row builtin='union(a, b)' arity='2' semantics='Concatenate then dedupe — set union by structural equality']
        [row builtin='intersect(a, b)' arity='2' semantics='Items present in both; structural-equality test']
        [row builtin='except(a, b)' arity='2' semantics='Items in a but not in b']
        [row builtin='flatten(seq)' arity='1' semantics='Flatten one level — Sequence-of-Array → Sequence-of-items']
        [row builtin='nth(seq, n)' arity='2' semantics='1-indexed item access; out-of-range raises CXER0100']
        [row builtin='position(seq, item)' arity='2' semantics='1-based index of first structural match; zero if absent']
        [row builtin='range(start, end)' arity='2' semantics='Inclusive integer sequence [start..end]; empty if end<start']
        [row builtin='identity(x)' arity='1' semantics='Returns x unchanged — for pipe stages and shape testing']
        [row builtin='sort-by(seq, key)' arity='2' semantics='Sort seq by key — key is a function or attribute reference']
        [row builtin='group-by(seq, key)' arity='2' semantics='Partition seq into groups keyed by key; yields a Map']
        [row builtin='take(seq, n)' arity='2' semantics='First n items; idempotent if n >= count(seq)']
        [row builtin='drop(seq, n)' arity='2' semantics='All items after the first n; empty if n >= count(seq)']
        [row builtin='some(seq, pred)' arity='2' semantics='Boolean — true iff any item satisfies pred']
        [row builtin='every(seq, pred)' arity='2' semantics='Boolean — true iff all items satisfy pred (vacuous true)']]
      [example lang=cx
        """[$count //user[@active=true]]
           [$sort-by //user @last-login]
           [$distinct //tag]
           [?pipe [$range 1 100] [$take _ 10]]
           [$some //user [?fn ($u) [= $u@role 'admin']]]"""]]

    [child id=string-builtins n=6.7.2 title='String built-ins'
      [body
        """Operate on a String scalar. Non-string scalars are
           stringified by the canonical scalar printer; element
           arguments raise `CXER0100` unless documented
           otherwise."""]
      [table columns=(builtin, arity, semantics)
        [row builtin='upper(s)' arity='1' semantics='Unicode upper-cased copy of s; alias: upcase']
        [row builtin='lower(s)' arity='1' semantics='Unicode lower-cased copy of s; alias: lowcase']
        [row builtin='trim(s)' arity='1' semantics='Whitespace stripped from both ends']
        [row builtin='concat(s1, s2, …)' arity='≥1' semantics='String concatenation; non-string scalars stringified']
        [row builtin='contains(s, sub)' arity='2' semantics='Boolean substring test; empty sub yields true']
        [row builtin='starts-with(s, prefix)' arity='2' semantics='Boolean prefix test; empty prefix yields true']
        [row builtin='ends-with(s, suffix)' arity='2' semantics='Boolean suffix test; empty suffix yields true']
        [row builtin='substring(s, start, len?)' arity='2–3' semantics='1-indexed substring; positions clamp; never raises']
        [row builtin='substring-before(s, sub)' arity='2' semantics='Prefix of s before first occurrence of sub']
        [row builtin='substring-after(s, sub)' arity='2' semantics='Suffix of s after first occurrence of sub']
        [row builtin='string-length(s)' arity='1' semantics='Integer count of Unicode codepoints (NOT bytes)']
        [row builtin='string-join(seq, sep)' arity='2' semantics='Join sequence of strings with separator']
        [row builtin='tokenize(s, sep)' arity='2' semantics='Split s on sep (regex); yields sequence of strings']
        [row builtin='split(s, sep)' arity='2' semantics='Synonym for tokenize with a literal (non-regex) separator']
        [row builtin='replace(s, find, repl)' arity='3' semantics='Replace all occurrences of find with repl']
        [row builtin='matches(s, regex)' arity='2' semantics='Boolean regex test (RE2 semantics)']
        [row builtin='normalize-space(s)' arity='1' semantics='Strip leading/trailing + collapse internal whitespace']
        [row builtin='format(template, args)' arity='2' semantics='Format string with positional args (e.g. {0}, {1})']
        [row builtin='text(elem)' arity='1' semantics='Body text of elem as string; concatenates text children']]
      [example lang=cx
        """[$upper 'hello world']                     [# → 'HELLO WORLD'  #]
           [$contains @email '@example.com']
           [$string-join (//user/@name) ', ']
           [$tokenize 'a,b,c' ',']                    [# → ('a', 'b', 'c') #]
           [$replace $body 'foo' 'bar']"""]]

    [child id=numeric-builtins n=6.7.3 title='Numeric built-ins'
      [body
        """Operate on numeric scalars. `sum`/`min`/`max`/`avg`
           operate on a sequence and skip non-numeric items;
           `floor`/`ceiling`/`round`/`abs` operate on a single
           value."""]
      [table columns=(builtin, arity, semantics)
        [row builtin='sum(seq)' arity='1' semantics='Sum of numeric scalars; empty yields int 0; any float → float result']
        [row builtin='min(seq)' arity='1' semantics='Minimum numeric; empty yields int 0']
        [row builtin='max(seq)' arity='1' semantics='Maximum numeric; empty yields int 0']
        [row builtin='avg(seq)' arity='1' semantics='Arithmetic mean as float; empty yields float 0.0']
        [row builtin='abs(x)' arity='1' semantics='Absolute value; preserves int/float kind']
        [row builtin='floor(x)' arity='1' semantics='Largest integer ≤ x; int passes through']
        [row builtin='ceiling(x)' arity='1' semantics='Smallest integer ≥ x; int passes through']
        [row builtin='round(x)' arity='1' semantics='Half-away-from-zero rounding (XPath fn:round)']
        [row builtin='mod(a, b)' arity='2' semantics='Modulo; sign follows dividend (C-style truncation)']
        [row builtin='pow(b, e)' arity='2' semantics='b raised to e; integer^integer stays int when possible']
        [row builtin='range(start, end)' arity='2' semantics='See sequence-builtins — duplicated here for discoverability']
        [row builtin='to-int(v)' arity='1' semantics='Coerce v to int; non-coercible raises CXER0103']
        [row builtin='to-float(v)' arity='1' semantics='Coerce v to float; non-coercible raises CXER0103']
        [row builtin='to-string(v)' arity='1' semantics='Coerce v to canonical string form']
        [row builtin='is-nan(x)' arity='1' semantics='Boolean — true iff x is the float NaN value']
        [row builtin='is-finite(x)' arity='1' semantics='Boolean — true iff x is finite (not nan/inf/-inf)']]
      [example lang=cx
        """[$sum //order/@amount]
           [$avg //user/@age]
           [$round 3.7]                               [# → 4              #]
           [$pow 2 10]                                [# → 1024           #]
           [$mod 17 5]                                [# → 2              #]"""]]

    [child id=logical-builtins n=6.7.4 title='Logical built-ins'
      [body
        """Boolean combinators and EBV-aware tests. `and` / `or`
           short-circuit; `not` is total over any EBV-typed
           argument."""]
      [table columns=(builtin, arity, semantics)
        [row builtin='not(v)' arity='1' semantics='Boolean negation under EBV (cxdm §4.6)']
        [row builtin='boolean(v)' arity='1' semantics='Coerce v to bool under EBV — explicit EBV evaluation']
        [row builtin='exists(v)' arity='1' semantics='Boolean — true iff v is non-empty (count > 0)']
        [row builtin='empty(v)' arity='1' semantics='Boolean — true iff v is the empty sequence']
        [row builtin='and(a, b, …)' arity='≥1' semantics='Boolean — true iff all args truthy; short-circuit L→R']
        [row builtin='or(a, b, …)' arity='≥1' semantics='Boolean — true iff any arg truthy; short-circuit L→R']
        [row builtin='xor(a, b)' arity='2' semantics='Boolean — true iff exactly one of a, b is truthy']
        [row builtin='if(c, t, e)' arity='3' semantics='Expression form of [?if] — c with EBV selects t or e']
        [row builtin='eq(a, b)' arity='2' semantics='Structural equality — types, values, attributes, children all match']
        [row builtin='ne(a, b)' arity='2' semantics='Structural inequality — convenience for not(eq(a, b))']
        [row builtin='instance-of(v, type)' arity='2' semantics='Boolean — true iff v has the given type (scalar kind or schema type)']]
      [example lang=cx
        """[$if @active 'green' 'gray']
           [and @verified @active [not @banned]]
           [$exists //error]                          [# any errors at all? #]
           [$instance-of @port ::u16]"""]]

    [child id=accessor-builtins n=6.7.5 title='Node-accessor built-ins'
      [body
        """Operate on an Element node — read structural
           properties. Script-level equivalents of the Layer-1
           binding methods (see [[layer-1]])."""]
      [table columns=(builtin, arity, semantics)
        [row builtin='name(elem)' arity='1' semantics='Element name as a string; non-element raises CXER0100']
        [row builtin='local-name(elem)' arity='1' semantics='Element name without namespace prefix']
        [row builtin='namespace-uri(elem)' arity='1' semantics='URI of the namespace bound to the element prefix, or empty string']
        [row builtin='kind(elem)' arity='1' semantics='Element / scalar / array / map / sequence — the cxdm Item kind']
        [row builtin='node-kind(elem)' arity='1' semantics='Synonym for kind — XPath fn:node-kind parity']
        [row builtin='attrs(elem)' arity='1' semantics='Map of attributes as {name: value} pairs']
        [row builtin='children(elem)' arity='1' semantics='Sequence of direct child elements (excludes scalar/text)']
        [row builtin='body(elem)' arity='1' semantics='Body content of elem — scalars, elements, mixed; preserves order']
        [row builtin='parent(elem)' arity='1' semantics='Direct parent, or empty sequence if elem is root']
        [row builtin='root(elem)' arity='1' semantics='Topmost ancestor — root of the containing document']
        [row builtin='id(name)' arity='1' semantics='Resolve an id-sigil reference to its target element']
        [row builtin='idref(elem)' arity='1' semantics='Inverse of id — elements referring to elem via id-sigil']
        [row builtin='position()' arity='0' semantics='Inside a predicate: 1-based position of context within its sequence']
        [row builtin='last()' arity='0' semantics='Inside a predicate: total count of items in the context sequence']
        [row builtin='current()' arity='0' semantics='The current context node — inside nested paths']
        [row builtin='document-uri(elem)' arity='1' semantics='URI of the document containing elem (file path or stream id)']]
      [example lang=cx
        """[$name //section[1]]                       [# → 'section'      #]
           [$kind $v]                                 [# → :scalar/:elem  #]
           [$children //user[1]]                      [# → seq of children#]
           //*[position() = last()]                   [# last child match #]"""]]]

  [child id=errors n=6.8 title='Errors as values'
    [intro
      """CX treats errors as ordinary values. An error is a CX
         element with kind `[err :code "cx-err:CXERnnnn" …]`. It
         can be passed around, pattern-matched, transformed, and
         recovered from. Full error registry in
         [`spec/code.md`](../../spec/code.md) §9.5."""]

    [body
      """**Propagation operators.** Two postfix operators thread
         errors through expressions:"""]
    [list
      [item """**`f($x)?`** — call `f`; if it errored, propagate
              the error unchanged through the surrounding
              directive (which catches it). This is the "early
              return" form."""]
      [item """**`f($x)!`** — call `f`; if it errored, raise
              immediately as a host exception (cross the
              CX/binding boundary). Use sparingly — it converts
              the error from a value to a runtime trap."""]]

    [example lang=cx
      """[?match [?let [= $u [$lookup-user $id]?] [$render $u]]
           [case [err code='CXER0204'] [err message='no such user']]
           [case [err code='CXER0163'] [err message='lookup timed out']]
           [else [err message='lookup failed']]]"""]

    [body
      """Recovery is `[?match]` (see [[match]]) over the `[err]`
         channel: the errorable expression is the scrutinee, and an
         `[err]` it produces is captured (not propagated) so a
         `[case [err …] …]` arm can dispatch on its code. Match by
         plain attribute equality (`[err code='CXER0204']`) or capture
         the code (`[err @code=$c]`). Arms evaluate first-match-wins; an
         `[else …]` (or bare `$err` capture) is the catch-all. With no
         catch-all and no match, the `[err]` propagates to the next
         enclosing `[?match]` or out of the program. For a plain
         value-or-default, `[?else EXPR DEFAULT]` (§8.13) is the
         lighter sugar; `[?fallback PRIMARY [recover-with …]]` (§10.2.4)
         binds `$err` in the recovery."""]

    [child id=error-registry n=6.8.1 title='Error code registry (CXER0100–0299)'
      [body
        """The CXER0100–0299 range is reserved for code-language
           errors. Errors are organized into subsystems by tens
           digits. The complete normative map is in
           [`spec/code.md`](../../spec/code.md) §9.5 — this
           subsection is the developer-facing summary."""]

      [body
        """**Pattern / parse — CXER010x:**"""]
      [table columns=(code, symbolic, meaning, recovery)
        [row code='CXER0100' symbolic='PARSE_ERROR' meaning='Pattern syntax invalid; directive name not in registry; arity mismatch' recovery='Fix source — this is author error, not runtime']
        [row code='CXER0101' symbolic='ANCHOR_ERROR' meaning='Duplicate &anchor; cyclic *merge; unresolved *name' recovery='Author error — flagged at parse time']
        [row code='CXER0102' symbolic='DUPLICATE_KEY' meaning='Map literal with duplicate key' recovery='Author error']
        [row code='CXER0103' symbolic='TYPE_ERROR' meaning='Value-comparison on multi-valued operand; sized-overflow; type-coerce failure' recovery='[?match] [case [err code="CXER0103"] …]; explicit coercion via to-int/to-float']]

      [body
        """**Resilience — CXER014x / CXER015x:**"""]
      [table columns=(code, symbolic, meaning, recovery)
        [row code='CXER0140' symbolic='RETRY_EXHAUSTED' meaning='[?retry] reached max= attempts without success' recovery='[?match] [case [err code="CXER0140"] …]; tune max= / backoff=']
        [row code='CXER0141' symbolic='TIMEOUT' meaning='[?timeout] DURATION elapsed' recovery='[on-timeout EXPR] or [?match] [case [err code="CXER0141"] …]']
        [row code='CXER0150' symbolic='BREAKER_OPEN' meaning='[?circuit-breaker] tripped; rejecting calls' recovery='Wait :reset DURATION; fallback path']
        [row code='CXER0151' symbolic='RATE_LIMITED' meaning='[?rate-limit] tokens exhausted' recovery='Honor :retry-after slot in the err; back off']
        [row code='CXER0152' symbolic='BULKHEAD_FULL' meaning='[?bulkhead] max-concurrent= + queue= exhausted' recovery='Backpressure caller; reduce concurrency']]

      [body
        """**Services — CXER016x:**"""]
      [table columns=(code, symbolic, meaning, recovery)
        [row code='CXER0160' symbolic='BAD_REQUEST' meaning='HTTP 400 — malformed body or path-param parse failure' recovery='Return [response status=400 body=err] to client']
        [row code='CXER0161' symbolic='UNAUTHORIZED' meaning='HTTP 401 — :auth returned err' recovery='Return 401; client re-authenticates']
        [row code='CXER0162' symbolic='NOT_FOUND' meaning='HTTP 404 — no resource matched path + method' recovery='Return 404; client checks URL']
        [row code='CXER0163' symbolic='REQUEST_TIMEOUT' meaning='HTTP 408 — :read-timeout exceeded' recovery='Retry with backoff; reduce request size']
        [row code='CXER0164' symbolic='PAYLOAD_TOO_LARGE' meaning='HTTP 413 — body > max-body-bytes=' recovery='Chunk upload; increase max-body-bytes= if appropriate']
        [row code='CXER0165' symbolic='INTERNAL_ERROR' meaning='HTTP 500 — unhandled err in resource body' recovery='Recover the body with [?match]/[?fallback]; log and return generic 500 to client']
        [row code='CXER0166' symbolic='SHUTTING_DOWN' meaning='HTTP 503 — service in shutdown drain' recovery='Client retries elsewhere; do not retry same instance']]

      [body
        """**Client — CXER018x:**"""]
      [table columns=(code, symbolic, meaning, recovery)
        [row code='CXER0180' symbolic='CONNECTION_REFUSED' meaning='TCP connect failure' recovery='Retry with backoff; check :target URL']
        [row code='CXER0181' symbolic='TLS_HANDSHAKE_FAILED' meaning='TLS negotiation failed' recovery='Check :tls config; cert validity; retry']
        [row code='CXER0182' symbolic='INVALID_RESPONSE' meaning='Server response could not be parsed' recovery='Log raw response; report upstream issue']]

      [body
        """**Concurrency — CXER020x / CXER022x:**"""]
      [table columns=(code, symbolic, meaning, recovery)
        [row code='CXER0200' symbolic='CHANNEL_CLOSED' meaning='Receive from drained-closed channel; send to closed channel' recovery='Treat as normal stream-end; [?match] on err code']
        [row code='CXER0201' symbolic='SEND_TIMEOUT' meaning='[?try-send] buffer-full timeout' recovery='Drop, queue elsewhere, or back off']
        [row code='CXER0202' symbolic='RECV_TIMEOUT' meaning='[?try-receive] empty timeout' recovery='Poll again; abandon work if pattern persists']
        [row code='CXER0203' symbolic='CHANNEL_ALREADY_CLOSED' meaning='Second [?close] on same channel' recovery='Idempotent close idiom — [?else [?close $ch] ()]']
        [row code='CXER0220' symbolic='WORKER_PANIC' meaning='Worker body raised unhandled err' recovery='Inspect cause= attribute; restart worker or supervisor pattern']
        [row code='CXER0221' symbolic='WORKER_CANCELLED' meaning='Worker terminated via [?cancel]' recovery='Expected on graceful shutdown; clean up resources']
        [row code='CXER0222' symbolic='WORKER_NOT_FOUND' meaning='[?worker-handle] lookup miss' recovery='Use [?worker-handle :name N :optional true] or check exists']]

      [body
        """**Async — CXER024x / CXER026x:**"""]
      [table columns=(code, symbolic, meaning, recovery)
        [row code='CXER0240' symbolic='AWAIT_ALL_FAILED' meaning='[?await-all] saw ≥ 1 non-done future' recovery='Inspect :causes slot — sequence of [err …] per failed future']
        [row code='CXER0241' symbolic='AWAIT_TIMEOUT' meaning='[?await $f :timeout] deadline expired (future itself still running)' recovery='Tune :timeout; abandon or [?cancel $f]']
        [row code='CXER0260' symbolic='CANCELLED' meaning='Operation observed cooperative cancellation' recovery='Expected; clean up; do not retry']]

      [body
        """**Visualization — CXER028x:**"""]
      [table columns=(code, symbolic, meaning, recovery)
        [row code='CXER0280' symbolic='RENDER_FAILED' meaning='Sequence-diagram renderer could not produce output' recovery='Check directive shape; report renderer version + program']
        [row code='CXER0281' symbolic='UNRENDERABLE_DIRECTIVE' meaning='Directive shape outside §10.1.2 locked render rules' recovery='Adjust source; renderer is conservative on purpose']]

      [body
        """The CXER0000–0099 range is reserved for data-language
           errors (parse, validate, canonicalize); see
           [[data-errors]] in the data section. CXER0300+ is
           reserved for future subsystems."""]]]

  [child id=concurrency n=6.9 title='Concurrency'
    [intro
      """CX has three concurrency surfaces, all spelled as
         directives. They build on the pure-functional core:
         every channel send, every worker call, every async
         await sees only immutable values, so there is no
         shared-mutable-state hazard. Spec:
         [`spec/code.md`](../../spec/code.md) §10.3–§10.5."""]

    [child id=services n=6.9.1 title='Services and clients'
      [body
        """`[?service]` declares a named HTTP service with
           resource handlers. `[?http-client]` is its client
           side. Resources handle requests and return
           responses; the bodies are ordinary CX code."""]

      [body
        """**Service definition:**"""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?service on=http port=8080 name='users'
           read-timeout=30s
           write-timeout=30s
           max-body-bytes=10485760            [# 10 MiB                  #]
           max-connections=1000
           grace-period=30s

           [resource method=GET path='/users/:id'
             [?let [= $u //user[@id=$request/path-params/id]]
               [response status=200 body=$u]]]

           [resource method=POST path='/users'
             consumes=application/cx
             [?let [= $body $request/body]
               [response status=201 body=[$create-user $body]]]]]"""]

      [body
        """**Resource parameters.** Each `[resource]` declares
           `method=METHOD path=PATH`, optional `produces=` /
           `consumes=` MIME attributes, optional `[auth …]`
           predicate-expression clause, and a body expression
           in positional position. PATH supports `:name`
           parameter binding — `/users/:id` exposes
           `$request/path-params/id` inside the body."""]

      [body
        """**Request and response shapes:**"""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[request method='GET' path='/users/42'
                   path-params={id: 42}
                   query-params={…}
                   headers=([header name='Accept' value='application/cx'])
                   body=$body]

           [response status=200
                     headers=(…)
                     body=$body]"""]

      [body
        """**Lifecycle.** `[?service]` returns a handle.
           `[?service-handle :name N]` looks up an existing
           service. `[?stop $handle]` initiates graceful
           shutdown — drains in-flight requests for up to
           `:grace-period`, then force-closes; new requests
           during drain receive HTTP 503 (`CXER0166`).
           `[?wait-for :service $handle]` blocks until
           termination. Process SIGTERM / SIGINT also triggers
           graceful shutdown."""]

      [body
        """**HTTP client.** `[?http-client :target URL …]`
           returns a client value; operations pipe through
           it:"""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?let [= $c [?http-client target='https://api.example.com'
                                    timeout=30s
                                    resilience=([?retry max=3])]]
             [?pipe $c [$get _ '/users/42']]]"""]

      [body
        """**Status.** Services are a v0.8.0-stable surface;
           Tier-1 V binding ships the full implementation.
           Tier-2 bindings expose `[?http-client]` and
           `[?service-handle]` lookup but service-hosting may
           defer to the host's native HTTP framework. See
           [[bindings]]."""]]

    [child id=workers n=6.9.2 title='Workers and channels'
      [body
        """`[?worker]` spawns a concurrent worker. `[?channel]`
           creates a FIFO message queue. `[?send]` and
           `[?receive]` move values across. `[?select]` is the
           multi-way receive."""]

      [example lang=cx
        """[?let [= $ch [?channel name='jobs' buffer=100]]
               [?worker name='producer'
                 [?for [in $i [$range 1 100]]
                       [yield [?send $i to=$ch]]]]
               [?worker name='consumer'
                 [?for [in $msg [$range 1 100]]
                       [yield [$process [?receive from=$ch]]]]]]"""]

      [body
        """**Channel semantics.** `[?channel :buffer N]` has
           a bounded FIFO buffer. `:buffer 0` is synchronous
           (rendezvous — send blocks until receive). FIFO
           ordering is preserved per producer-consumer pair;
           with multiple producers, per-producer FIFO is
           preserved but there is no global cross-producer
           ordering."""]

      [body
        """**Send / receive variants.**"""]
      [list
        [item """`[?send V to=CH]` — block when buffer full;
                returns `[ok]` once buffered. Returns
                `CXER0200` (CHANNEL_CLOSED) if CH is
                closed."""]
        [item """`[?try-send V to=CH timeout=DURATION]` —
                same but returns `CXER0201` (SEND_TIMEOUT) on
                buffer-full timeout."""]
        [item """`[?receive from=CH]` — block when empty;
                returns next value. Returns `CXER0200` when CH
                is closed and drained."""]
        [item """`[?try-receive from=CH timeout=DURATION]` —
                same but returns `CXER0202` (RECV_TIMEOUT) on
                empty-channel timeout."""]
        [item """`[?close CH]` — mark closed. Subsequent
                `[?send]` fails immediately; subsequent
                `[?receive]` drains buffered values then
                returns CHANNEL_CLOSED. Double-close raises
                `CXER0203`."""]]

      [body
        """**Worker observability.** Workers are
           handle-addressable:"""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?let [= $w [?worker name='sync-job' [$sync-database]]]
               [?wait-for worker=$w]                   [# block until done #]
               [?cancel worker=$w]                     [# cooperative      #]
               [?check-cancel]                         [# polling point    #]]"""]

      [body
        """**Select — multi-way receive.** Evaluates all
           `[case …]` clauses concurrently; proceeds with the
           first ready. Uniform-random selection on
           simultaneous readiness:"""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?select
           [case [from $jobs   $job [$handle-job $job]]]
           [case [from $signal $sig [$handle-signal $sig]]]
           [case [timeout 5s        [$heartbeat]]]]"""]
      [note
        """**Shape.** `[?select]` clauses are clause-children:
           `[case [from CH $msg HANDLER]]` for a receive and
           `[case [timeout DURATION HANDLER]]` for a deadline. The
           spec/core/code.md §10.4.7 registry holds the
           authoritative shape. The v0.7.x `:case [:from CH $msg H]`
           colon-slot form is retired — there is no dual-accept."""]]

    [child id=async n=6.9.3 title='Async, await, and cancellation'
      [body
        """`[?async EXPR]` evaluates EXPR in a new async
           context and returns a future immediately:"""]

      [example lang=cx
        """[?let [= $f1 [?async [$fetch $url1]]]
                [= $f2 [?async [$fetch $url2]]]
                [= $f3 [?async [$fetch $url3]]]
                [?await-all $f1 $f2 $f3]]"""]

      [body
        """**Future state machine.** Futures progress through
           `pending → running → done` / `failed` /
           `cancelled`. Once terminal, the state is
           immutable."""]

      [body
        """**Barrier variants:**"""]
      [list
        [item """`[?await $f]` — block until terminal; return
                value on done, propagate err on failed,
                CANCELLED on cancelled."""]
        [item """`[?await $f :timeout DURATION]` — add a hard
                deadline; on expiry return `CXER0241`
                (AWAIT_TIMEOUT). The future continues running
                — `:timeout` bounds the caller's wait, not the
                future itself."""]
        [item """`[?await-all FUTURES]` — block until all
                terminal. If all done, yield sequence of
                values. If any failed/cancelled, yield
                `CXER0240` with `:causes` slot containing
                every non-done cause in input order."""]
        [item """`[?await-any FUTURES]` — yield first to
                terminate. Other futures continue running;
                their results are discarded."""]
        [item """`[?await-race FUTURES]` — yield first to
                terminate; **other futures are cancelled**.
                Use for "I only need one"."""]]

      [body
        """**Cooperative cancellation contract.** `[?cancel
           $f]` requests cancellation — it is a signal, not
           a kill. The runtime guarantees cancellation
           observation at:"""]
      [list
        [item """HTTP client calls at next network read/write
                boundary."""]
        [item """`[?sleep DURATION]` — immediately."""]
        [item """`[?send]` / `[?receive]` / `[?try-send]` /
                `[?try-receive]` — immediately, returning
                `CXER0260` (CANCELLED)."""]
        [item """`[?for]` — at every iteration boundary."""]
        [item """`[?await]` — immediately."""]]

      [body
        """**Pure CPU loops without yield points do NOT
           observe cancellation.** Authors who want a
           cancellable hot loop must insert `[?check-cancel]`
           at appropriate boundaries — it evaluates to `[ok]`
           normally, or `CXER0260` (CANCELLED) if
           cancellation is pending."""]

      [example lang=cx
        """[?def cancellable-compute ($n)
             [?for [in $i [$range 1 $n]]
               [where [= [?check-cancel] [ok]]]
               [yield [$expensive-op $i]]]]"""]

      [body
        """**Composition with resilience.** `[?async]`
           composes with every resilience directive.
           `[?async [?timeout 5s [$slow-call]]]` returns
           a future that resolves to `CXER0141` (TIMEOUT)
           after 5s if `slow-call` hasn't completed."""]

      [body
        """**Status.** Async/await is v0.8.0-stable in V.
           Tier-2 binding parity (Python `asyncio`, Go
           `chan`, Rust `tokio`) ships per
           [`spec/v0_8_0_status.md`](../../spec/v0_8_0_status.md)
           gate 28."""]]]

  [child id=resilience n=6.10 title='Resilience directives'
    [intro
      """The six resilience directives wrap an inner
         expression with a failure-handling policy. They are
         not new control-flow primitives — they are normal CX
         code with cleanly-named policies. Spec:
         [`spec/code.md`](../../spec/code.md) §10.2."""]

    [body
      """**Common error shape.** Every resilience directive
         on terminal failure emits a structured `[err …]`
         per the §10.2.7 error-code matrix (see
         [[error-registry]])."""]

    [child id=retry n=6.10.1 title='[?retry] — bounded re-run with backoff'
      [body
        """Re-run an inner body on `[err …]` return, up to
           `max=` attempts, sleeping between attempts per
           `backoff=` / `delay=` / `jitter=`. Terminal
           failure raises `CXER0140` (RETRY_EXHAUSTED)."""]
      [table columns=(parameter, default, semantics)
        [row parameter='max=INT' default='(required)' semantics='Maximum attempts; must be > 0']
        [row parameter='backoff=STRATEGY' default='exponential' semantics='constant / linear / exponential / fibonacci']
        [row parameter='delay=DURATION' default='100ms' semantics='Base delay; combined with backoff formula']
        [row parameter='jitter=MODE' default='equal' semantics='none / full / equal / decorrelated']
        [row parameter='[on PREDICATE]' default='[?fn ($e) true]' semantics='Per-err filter; retry iff truthy']
        [row parameter='body (positional)' default='(required)' semantics='Inner expression to retry']]
      [body
        """**Backoff formulas** (delay for attempt N, N ≥ 1):
           `constant` = `delay=`; `linear` = `delay × N`;
           `exponential` = `delay × 2^(N-1)`; `fibonacci` =
           `delay × fib(N)`."""]
      [body
        """**Jitter modes** (D = pre-jitter delay): `none` =
           `D`; `full` = `uniform(0, D)`; `equal` = `D/2 +
           uniform(0, D/2)`; `decorrelated` = `uniform(delay,
           last_delay × 3)`."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?retry max=5 backoff=exponential delay=200ms jitter=equal
           [on [?fn ($e) [= $e@code 'CXER0163']]]        [# only on timeout #]
           [?pipe $client [$get _ '/users/42']]]"""]]

    [child id=timeout n=6.10.2 title='[?timeout] — hard deadline'
      [body
        """Bound execution time of an inner body. On expiry,
           cooperatively cancel the body and return
           the `[on-timeout …]` clause body (if present) or
           `[result status=err code='cx-err:CXER0141'
           elapsed=DURATION]`."""]
      [table columns=(parameter, default, semantics)
        [row parameter='DURATION (positional)' default='(required)' semantics='Hard deadline; e.g. 5s, 250ms']
        [row parameter='body (positional)' default='(required)' semantics='Inner expression with cooperative cancel points']
        [row parameter='[on-timeout EXPR]' default='(none)' semantics='Fallback expression on expiry; otherwise raises CXER0141']]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?timeout 5s
             [$slow-database-query $q]
             [on-timeout [result status=err code='CXER0141' reason='db slow']]]"""]]

    [child id=circuit-breaker n=6.10.3 title='[?circuit-breaker] — fail-fast on consecutive failures'
      [body
        """Three states: **closed** (passes through), **open**
           (rejects with `CXER0150` (BREAKER_OPEN) without
           calling `:body`), **half-open** (allows one
           probe). Transitions:"""]
      [list
        [item """Closed → open when failure ratio in rolling
                `window=` exceeds `threshold=`, with at least
                `min-samples=` recorded."""]
        [item """Open → half-open after `reset=` elapses."""]
        [item """Half-open → closed if probe succeeds."""]
        [item """Half-open → open if probe fails."""]]
      [table columns=(parameter, default, semantics)
        [row parameter='threshold=RATIO' default='(required)' semantics='Failure ratio 0.0–1.0 to trip breaker']
        [row parameter='window=DURATION' default='(required)' semantics='Rolling-window length for sampling']
        [row parameter='reset=DURATION' default='(required)' semantics='Half-open delay after trip']
        [row parameter='min-samples=INT' default='10' semantics='Minimum sample count before threshold check']
        [row parameter='name=STR' default='(lexical position)' semantics='State-identity key for cross-position sharing']
        [row parameter='body (positional)' default='(required)' semantics='Inner expression']]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?circuit-breaker
             threshold=0.5 window=60s reset=30s min-samples=20
             name='users-api'
             [?pipe $client [$get _ '/users/42']]]"""]]

    [child id=fallback n=6.10.4 title='[?fallback] — alternative on error'
      [body
        """If primary returns `[err …]`, evaluate secondary
           and return its value (which may itself be `[err]`
           — `[?fallback]` does not wrap that case)."""]
      [table columns=(parameter, default, semantics)
        [row parameter='body (positional)' default='(required)' semantics='First expression tried (primary)']
        [row parameter='[recover-with SECONDARY]' default='(required)' semantics='Fallback expression evaluated only on primary err']]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?fallback
             [?pipe $client [$get _ '/users/42']]
             [recover-with [$load-from-cache 42]]]"""]
      [note
        """**`$err` in `[recover-with …]`.** Inside the recovery
           scope, `$err` is bound to the primary's `[err …]` value, so
           recovery can introspect the failure (e.g. `[recover-with
           [response status=500 reason=$err/@message]]`). The
           clause-child `[recover-with …]` form is the sole accepted
           shape (no legacy colon-slot)."""]]

    [child id=rate-limit n=6.10.5 title='[?rate-limit] — token-bucket cap'
      [body
        """Permits at most `max=` invocations per `per=`
           window. Excess invocations return `CXER0151`
           (RATE_LIMITED) with a `retry-after=DURATION`
           attribute — the time until the next token frees."""]
      [table columns=(parameter, default, semantics)
        [row parameter='max=INT' default='(required)' semantics='Tokens per window; must be > 0']
        [row parameter='per=DURATION' default='(required)' semantics='Refill window']
        [row parameter='name=STR' default='(lexical position)' semantics='State-identity key']
        [row parameter='body (positional)' default='(required)' semantics='Inner expression']]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?rate-limit max=100 per=1s name='github-api'
             [?pipe $client [$get _ '/repos/cx-home/cx']]]"""]]

    [child id=bulkhead n=6.10.6 title='[?bulkhead] — bound concurrent calls'
      [body
        """Bounds concurrent invocations. If `max-concurrent=`
           slots are busy and `queue=` slots are full,
           returns `CXER0152` (BULKHEAD_FULL). Queued
           requests wait FIFO for a free slot."""]
      [table columns=(parameter, default, semantics)
        [row parameter='max-concurrent=INT' default='(required)' semantics='In-flight cap; must be > 0']
        [row parameter='queue=INT' default='0' semantics='Wait-queue depth; 0 = no queue, fail-fast on full']
        [row parameter='name=STR' default='(lexical position)' semantics='State-identity key']
        [row parameter='body (positional)' default='(required)' semantics='Inner expression']]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?bulkhead max-concurrent=10 queue=50 name='db-pool'
             [$query-database $q]]"""]]

    [child id=resilience-composition n=6.10.7 title='Composition'
      [body
        """Resilience directives compose by nesting. The
           outermost runs first, so its policy sees the
           inner policies' err codes. Standard pattern reads
           top-down:"""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?retry max=3
            [?timeout 10s
              [?circuit-breaker threshold=0.5 window=60s reset=30s
                [?http-client target='https://api.example.com' method='get']]]]"""]
      [body
        """Reading: retry up to 3 times a 10-second-bounded
           call gated by a 50%-failure circuit-breaker. If
           the breaker opens, `[?retry]` sees `CXER0150` and
           decides whether to keep retrying (default `[on …]`
           predicate returns truthy → yes; override to scope
           which errs retry)."""]
      [body
        """**State identity (stateful directives).**
           `[?circuit-breaker]`, `[?rate-limit]`, and
           `[?bulkhead]` carry state across invocations. By
           default state is keyed by lexical source-text
           position — every evaluation of the same source
           node (including iterated under `[?for]` or
           `[?map par=true]`) shares state. Pass `name=STR`
           to key by name instead, enabling cross-position
           sharing (one rate-limit budget enforced by
           callers in different modules)."""]]]

  [# Migrated from docs-src/content/concepts/evaluation.cx and
     docs-src/content/reference/code.cx during Phase B docs → guide
     consolidation (2026-05-24). Higher-level framing of how
     directives, patterns, and comprehensions compose. #]

  [child id=evaluation-framing n=6.11 title='Evaluation framing — directives, patterns, comprehensions'
    [intro
      """The directive registry above lists each form; this section
         shows how the forms fit together — directives as the
         language layer over data, pattern matching as the way
         programs walk a tree, comprehensions as the composition
         glue. CXPath is a value kind (with `[?find]` retired) and
         `[expr]` is the general predicate."""]

    [child id=directives-overview n=6.11.1 title='Directives turn data into a language'
      [body
        """A directive is a CX element whose name starts with `?`.
           It carries semantics — iteration, branching,
           substitution, include, function call — that the
           evaluator interprets when running a CX program.
           Directives parse as regular CX, evaluate to text or
           values, and compose with each other through their slot
           bodies."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """EXPR                                     # bare expression evaluates
           [?for [in $x PATH] [yield BODY]]         # iterate
           [?if COND [then THEN] [else ELSE]]       # branch
           [?let [= $x EXPR] BODY]                  # bind a name
           [?def name ($a $b) BODY]                 # define a function"""]]

    [child id=patterns-walking n=6.11.2 title='Pattern matching — walking the tree'
      [body
        """The v0.8.0 program surface replaces XPath-style queries
           with `[?for]` pattern generators and CXPath path values
           (`[?find]` retired). A pattern is a CX
           literal where element names match by shape, attribute
           predicates restrict by value, and `$bindings` capture
           matched subtrees. `[?for]` walks the tree (deep by
           default) and binds for each match; `[?match]` tests a
           single value (multi-arm dispatch)."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?for [user [name $n]] [yield $n]]
           [?for [item @active=true $i] [yield $i]]
           [?for [pizza [name $n] [price $p]]
                 [yield [hit name=$n price=$p]]]"""]]

    [child id=comprehensions n=6.11.3 title='Comprehensions and composition'
      [body
        """`[?for]` iterates a sequence (or matches a pattern across
           the tree) and yields per-iteration values; `[where …]`
           filters, `[order-by …]` sorts, `[group-by …]` aggregates.
           `[?let]` binds names; `[?if]` branches. The `[?pipe]`
           directive composes them."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?for [in $x (1, 2, 3)] [where [> $x 1]] [yield [* $x $x]]]
           [?let [= $a 10] [?let [= $b 32] [+ $a $b]]]
           [?pipe (1, 2, 3, 4) count]"""]

      [child id=comprehensions-future-surface n=6.11.3.1
             title='Future surface — ranges, slices, Iterator'
        [body
          """A future surface extends the
             comprehension surface with **strided ranges**
             (`[$range 1 99 2]`, `[$range 1 *]`), **slice expressions on a
             binding postfix** (`$xs[2:5]`, `$xs[::-1]`,
             `$xs[:, "name"]`), **first-class slice values** (`[?def
             $s [2:$_last:2]]`), and a new **`Iterator` value
             kind** in CXDM §2 alongside Sequence / Array / Map.
             Comprehensions become lazy by default; named iterators
             (`[?def`]) memoize on first walk so re-use is safe."""]

        [body
          """Eight worked examples (§W1–§W8) each have a paired
             conformance fixture
             `program-slice-wN-*` in
             [`conformance/code.txt`](../../conformance/code.txt);
             until the implementation lands the fixtures are
             `out_err: cx-err:CXER0100` (parse-deferred), then flip
             to `out_text` in a single commit per fixture. The
             reconciliation test
             `program-cxpath-fncall-slice-composition`
             verifies grammar disambiguation against the B15
             closure (`spec/cxpath.md` §2.2) — bare-fn-call, path
             predicate, and slice postfix coexist."""]

        [example lang=cx
          """[# W1 — strided range, then slice, then comprehension #]
             [?def $odds [$range 1 99 2]]
             [?def $first-ten $odds[:10]]
             [?def $squares [?for [in $x $first-ten] [yield [* $x $x]]]]

             [# W5 — infinite range with [take …] #]
             [?for [in $x [$range 1 *]] [where [$prime? $x]] [take 100] [yield $x]]

             [# W7 — map comprehension over CXPath source #]
             [?for [in $u //user] [yield-map [$u@id $u@email]]]"""]

        [body
          """The path-to-iterator taxonomy (D26): a CXPath value is
             **not** an `Iterator` — it is a *description of a
             selection*. Evaluation materialises it to a `sequence`
             (eager, the kind that ships in v0.8.0). The `sequence`
             flows into Iterator combinators on opt-in via `[?for]`
             or the explicit `[?to-iterator]` directive. There is no
             `iterator → path` direction — paths describe; iterators
             walk."""]]]]

  [# Migrated from docs-src/content/reference/{cxpath,errors}.cx during
     Phase B docs → guide consolidation (2026-05-24). CXPath
     reference complements §6.3; errors complements §6.8. #]

  [child id=cxpath-quickref n=6.12 title='CXPath reference — quick form'
    [intro
      """At-a-glance CXPath reference. The normative grammar lives
         in [`spec/grammar.ebnf`](../../spec/grammar.ebnf)
         productions [130]-[135] (path / predicate base) and
         [159]-[160] (general PredicateExpr + `:bind` step
         modifier). XPath 3.1 alignment and divergences are
         tabulated in
         [`spec/cxpath_alignment.md`](../../spec/cxpath_alignment.md).
         CXPath is a first-class value kind at v0.8.0;
         general predicates with `$_` / `$_position` / `$_last`."""]

    [child id=cxpath-quick-table n=6.12.1 title='Quick reference'
      [example lang=cx
        """# Every user in the document.
           //user

           # Every user whose @active attribute equals true.
           //user[@active=true]

           # Email addresses of every active user.
           //user[@active=true]/@email

           # The third user in document order.
           //user[3]

           # Anything two levels deep under root.
           /*/*

           # Every sibling that follows the first user.
           //user[1]/following-sibling::user

           # Every ancestor element of a [para] node.
           //para/ancestor::*

           # General predicate — explicit $_ context binding.
           //user[$_@age >= 18 and $_@active]

           # Cross-step :bind — name the outer focus, reference from inner predicate.
           //team :bind t / member[$_@role = "lead" and count($t/member) >= 3]

           # Range and sequence ops — XPath 3.1 idioms.
           [$range 1 10]
           //a union //b
           //x intersect //y
           //all except //banned"""]]

    [child id=cxpath-four-forms n=6.12.2 title='Four leading forms'
      [body
        """Every CXPath has one of four leading-token forms.
           - **`//` descendant** — `//user` —
             `descendant-or-self::node()/child::user`; the most
             common shape.
           - **`/` absolute** — `/root/item` — child of the
             document root; rare in well-shaped data.
           - **relative** — `user/email` — applied against the
             current evaluation context.
           - **`$name/` binding** — `$u/email` — rooted at a
             previously-bound value (per `[?for]` / `[?let]` /
             `:bind`)."""]]

    [child id=cxpath-axes n=6.12.3 title='Twelve axes'
      [body
        """Every step can specify an axis. The default axis is
           `child::`; the `attribute::` axis has the dedicated
           `@` sigil. CX implements all twelve
           XPath 3.1 axes: `child`, `descendant`,
           `descendant-or-self`, `parent`, `ancestor`,
           `ancestor-or-self`, `self`, `following-sibling`,
           `preceding-sibling`, `following`, `preceding`,
           `attribute`."""]
      [body
        """Composite shortcut forms: `/name` ≡ `child::name`;
           `//name` ≡ `descendant-or-self::node()/child::name`;
           `@name` ≡ `attribute::name`; `..` ≡ `parent::node()`;
           `.` ≡ `self::node()`. Attribute axis is name-disjoint
           from element axes — `//user/@name` selects the
           attribute only; `//user/name` selects the child
           element only."""]]

    [child id=cxpath-predicates n=6.12.4 title='General predicate + reserved bindings'
      [body
        """Predicates filter the step sequence. v0.8.0 ratified
           the `[expr]` general predicate: every
           predicate body is an arbitrary CX expression coerced
           to a boolean via the Effective Boolean Value (EBV)
           rule. Atomic forms — `[@name]`, `[@name=V]`, `[N]`,
           `[fn(…)]` — remain available as parse-time sugar and
           round-trip via canonical re-emit when the AST matches
           a recognised template."""]
      [body
        """**Three reserved bindings** in scope inside every
           predicate body: `$_` (current candidate item, type
           matches the candidate kind), `$_position` (1-based int
           index — equivalent to XPath `position()`), `$_last`
           (int cardinality of the candidate sequence —
           equivalent to XPath `last()`). Predicates are eagerly
           materialised, so `$_last` is known on
           every predicate evaluation."""]
      [body
        """**Comparison sigils** are value-comparison only: `=`,
           `!=`, `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`. No keyword synonyms (no
           `eq`/`ne`/`lt`/etc.). Comparing a sequence with more
           than one item raises `CXER0103`. **Purity** — every
           predicate body MUST be pure; impure
           callees fail load with `CXER0230`."""]]

    [child id=cxpath-bind n=6.12.5 title=':bind peer-modifier'
      [body
        """`:bind NAME` is a peer-modifier slot on a path step,
           appearing between the NodeTest and any trailing
           predicates. At most one `:bind` per step. The name
           binds the step's focus value under the given
           identifier, and the binding is visible in every
           predicate enclosed by that step and in every
           subsequent step of the same PathExpr."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """//team :bind t / member[ $_@role = "lead" and count($t/member) >= 3 ]

           # $t — the team the path is currently resolving
           # $_ — the current member (rebinds at each predicate)"""]
      [note
        """`:bind _` is rejected with `CXER0232` (reserved bind
           name). `:bind` participates in the closed
           modifier-keyword set for the path-step
           disambiguation rule."""]]

    [child id=cxpath-binding-surface n=6.12.6 title='In the host bindings'
      [body
        """Every Tier-1 binding exposes CXPath identically through
           its Layer-1 surface — same selector, same bytes
           returned across the C ABI."""]
      [example lang=python """emails = doc.select_all("//user[@active=true]/@email")"""]
      [example lang=go      """emails, _ := doc.SelectAll("//user[@active=true]/@email")"""]
      [example lang=rust    """let emails = doc.select_all("//user[@active=true]/@email")?;"""]
      [example lang=v       """emails := doc.select_all('//user[@active=true]/@email')!"""]]]

  [child id=errors-catalog n=6.13 title='Error code catalog (v0.8.0)'
    [intro
      """Every CX error carries a structured payload: `cx-err:CODE`
         then a unit separator (US, `0x1F`) then a human-readable
         description then optionally another unit separator and a
         value. Bindings preserve this shape so `[?match]` can match
         on code rather than on text. The catalog below
         covers the v0.8.0 ranges; the full normative table lives
         in [`spec/errors.md`](../../spec/errors.md)."""]

    [child id=errors-security n=6.13.1 title='CXER001x — security caps'
      [body
        """- `CXER0010` — function call depth exceeded.
           - `CXER0011` — collection size cap reached
             (`sequence_len`, `map_entries`, `capture_size`).
           - `CXER0014` — eval-time include attempted without
             `--include-root`."""]]

    [child id=errors-eval-gates n=6.13.2 title='CXER004x — evaluator gates'
      [body
        """- `CXER0040` — purity gate: `SideEffect` /
             `ReadOnly` filter raised under `[?cx pure-only]`.
           - `CXER0041` — module gate: `on_declaration` module
             called without `use-module`.
           - `CXER0042` — eval gate: `cx:eval` invoked without
             `[?cx allow-eval]`.
           - `CXER0043` — eval depth gate: `cx:eval` recursion
             exceeded `max-eval-depth`."""]]

    [child id=errors-include n=6.13.3 title='E9xx — include resolver'
      [body
        """- `E901` — absolute path rejected.
           - `E902` — path escapes include root (lexical or
             symlink).
           - `E903` — URL-scheme path rejected.
           - `E904` — cycle detected.
           - `E905` — max depth exceeded.
           - `E906` — file not found.
           - `E907` — file not readable.
           - `E908` — path is a directory.
           - `E909` — I/O error during read.
           - `E910` — non-UTF-8 content.
           - `E911` — included file parse failure."""]]

    [child id=errors-catching n=6.13.4 title='Recovering with [?match]'
      [example lang=cx
        """[?match [$read-file $path]
             [case [err @code=$c] [where [= $c 'E906']] [file-missing]]
             [case [err $e] [other-error code=$e/@code]]
             [case $v $v]]"""]]]]
